---
/**
 * The keymap, rendered from the release's `docs.json` rather than typed out
 * here. bushel's cheatsheet is the source, so this page cannot drift the way
 * the README's hand-copied keymap did — it had lost four bindings by the time
 * the docs site was charted.
 *
 * Decided in https://github.com/frankieramirez/bushel/issues/47
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { bushelDocs } from "../../lib/bushelDocs";

const { version, keymap } = await bushelDocs();

/** Group names are already `global` / `list` / `detail`; slugged defensively. */
const slug = (group: string) =>
  group
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-")
    .replace(/^-|-$/g, "");

const headings = keymap.map((g) => ({
  depth: 2,
  slug: slug(g.group),
  text: g.group,
}));
headings.push({ depth: 2, slug: "contextual-keys", text: "Contextual keys" });
---

<Docs
  title="Keys"
  description="bushel's generated cheatsheet, plus controls for prompts, settings and other screens."
  lede="Every binding in bushel's help overlay, plus the keys used by its other prompts."
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    Press <code>?</code> inside bushel for this same list without leaving the terminal.
    The table is generated from bushel v{version} and matches the rows in its help
    overlay. The overlay closes with <code>esc</code>, <code>q</code>,
    <code>?</code>, or <code>m</code>; <code>q</code> and <code>esc</code> also
    appear in the global and detail rows below.
  </p>

  {
    keymap.map((group) => (
      <>
        <h2 id={slug(group.group)}>{group.group}</h2>
        <div class="docs-scroll">
          <table class="docs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Keys</th>
                <th>Does</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {group.bindings.map((binding) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{binding.keys}</code>
                  </td>
                  <td>{binding.desc}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <p>
    The groups describe what each binding acts on.
    <code>j</code>/<code>k</code> and <code>g</code>/<code>G</code> move the
    selection while the list is focused, then scroll the detail pane once
    <code>enter</code> has moved focus there. <code>l</code> and <code>i</code>
    switch the detail tab without leaving the list, though they need the
    containers pane; <code>F</code> also needs the Logs tab whose follow it
    toggles.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contextual-keys">Contextual keys</h2>

  <p>
    Some keys belong to a prompt or screen instead of the help table. In a
    confirmation prompt, <code>y</code> runs the action and <code>n</code> or
    <code>esc</code> cancels it. Pull, tag, and create-volume dialogs accept
    text, <code>enter</code> submits it, <code>backspace</code> deletes a
    character, and <code>esc</code> closes the dialog.
  </p>

  <p>
    After <code>/</code> starts a list filter, type to narrow the active pane,
    use <code>backspace</code> to edit, <code>enter</code> to leave filter input,
    or <code>esc</code> to clear it. In the settings panel opened with
    <code>,</code>, <code>j</code>/<code>k</code> or the up/down arrows move between
    rows, and <code>enter</code>, <code>space</code>, <code>h</code>,
    <code>l</code>, or the left and right arrows change the selected setting.
    <code>q</code>, <code>,</code>, or <code>esc</code> closes that panel.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>Ctrl-C</code> quits from any screen. If the container service is down,
    <code>s</code> starts it, <code>m</code> opens the message log, and
    <code>q</code> quits.
  </p>

  <p>
    The keys that need the list focused are <code>/</code>, <code>enter</code>,
    <code>space</code> and the action letters. Those letters are filtered to what
    the selection can actually do, so one that appears inert usually means the
    action does not apply here. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>
    covers the rest of what can look broken.
  </p>
</Docs>
